To understand how Git tracks your files, it helps to think of Git as a three-tier architecture with a fourth permanent storage step. Your files move through these distinct sections
or "stages" as you write code and save snapshots.

The 4 Stages of Git:

 [ 1. Working Directory ]  -->  [ 2. Staging Area ]  -->  [ 3. Local Repo ]  -->  [ 4. Remote Repo ]
   (Edits/Untracked)             (git add)                  (git commit)             (git push)

1. Working Directory (Untracked / Modified)
   A. What it is: The actual folder on your computer where you create, delete, and edit files.
   B. Git's Status: Git sees that changes exist here, but they are not safely saved in your history yet.

2. Staging Area (Index)
   A. What it is: A digital "loading dock" or a rough draft of your next snapshot.
   B. Git's Status: You choose exactly which changes from your working directory you want to include in your next save.

3. Local Repository (Committed)
   A. What it is: Your permanent, local "time machine" history database stored inside the hidden .git folder.
   B. Git's Status: The changes are safely snapshotted on your computer with a unique ID and a commit message.

4. Remote Repository (Pushed)
   A. What it is: A copy of your project history hosted on the cloud (like GitHub or GitLab).
   B. Git's Status: Your local snapshots are uploaded online for safekeeping and collaboration.

The Commands That Move Files Between Stages

git status (The Inspector)
 A. Does not move files.
 B. Checks your project and tells you exactly which stage your files are currently sitting in.
 C. Color cue: Untracked/modified files usually show up in red, while staged files show up in green.

git add <file> (Move to Staging)
 A. Moves a file from the Working Directory into the Staging Area.
 B. Use git add . to stage all changed files at once.

git commit -m "message" (Move to Local Repo)
 A. Packages everything currently sitting in the Staging Area and saves it permanently to the Local Repository.
 B. Clears out the staging area so it is empty for your next batch of work.

git push (Move to Remote Repo)
 A. Uploads all your locally Committed changes up to the Remote Repository.

Note: If a file is just added but not committed, and later deleted, it cannot be restored. Only if a file is committed (tracked), it can be restored if deleted by mistake.
